title "Statistical inference"
layout layered dir=down
page a3
font 8pt..36pt wrap=17em

style data color=#1565C0
style method color=#EF6C00
style conclusion color=#6A1B9A

node population "Population" .data
| all units of interest
| parameter describes the population, usually unknown
node sample "Random sample" .data
| observed subset
| probability sampling supports generalization
node design "Study design" .method
| sampling, controls, and measurements
| randomization separates treatment from confounding
node data "Observed data" .data
| measurements plus missingness and recording choices
| plots reveal structure before modeling
node estimate "Estimate" .method
| statistic used to learn about a parameter
| bias concerns accuracy; variance concerns spread
node model "Probability model" .method
| assumptions connect data to unknown parameters
| likelihood describes data conditional on parameters
node uncertainty "Uncertainty" .method
| standard error, confidence interval, or posterior
| more data often narrows sampling uncertainty
node hypothesis "Hypothesis test" .method
| compare evidence with a null model
| p-value is not the probability the null is true
node effect "Effect size" .method
| magnitude matters alongside significance
| practical importance requires domain context
node conclusion "Scientific conclusion" .conclusion
| report effect size, uncertainty, and limitations
| distinguish association from causal evidence
node replicate "Replication" .conclusion
| test whether the result generalizes
| new samples expose fragile conclusions
node bias "Bias and confounding" .conclusion
| selection, measurement, and omitted-variable bias
| a precise biased estimate is still wrong
node bayes "Bayesian updating" .conclusion
| posterior proportional to likelihood times prior
| prior assumptions must be reported

population -> sample
sample -> design
sample -> data
design -> estimate
design -> model
data -> estimate
estimate -> uncertainty
model -> uncertainty
uncertainty -> hypothesis
uncertainty -> effect
hypothesis -> conclusion
effect -> conclusion
conclusion -> replicate
replicate ..> population "generalizes back"
bias ..> design "audit"
bayes ..> model "alternative framework"

require population at top
require conclusion at bottom
prefer replicate near population within=18em weight=2
prefer bias near design within=18em weight=2
